    {
      "id": "FIN-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-flag-correlation",
      "title": "A nonpositive correlation on one double coset controls the averaging norm",
      "statement": "In G=Kᵣ/core(B), suppose f is real, left E-invariant and has mean zero. The correlation c(s)=mean_k f(sk)f(k) equals ‖f‖² on E and has a single nonpositive value elsewhere. The mean of c over E′ is the squared norm of the E′-average of f; at most the fraction |E∩E′|/|E′| contributes positively. Pairing with any E′-invariant g therefore has absolute value at most [E′:B]⁻¹ᐟ²‖f‖‖g‖, hence at most one fifth at the selected size.",
      "hypotheses": "E∩E′=B has finite index in Kᵣ; all groups in the formula may be replaced by their images modulo core(B). The chosen E has two double cosets. In arithmetic L² the first vector must be orthogonal to all Kᵣ-invariants, so a representative has zero average over the finite fiber at every base point. Haar unfolding and integration preserve the bound.",
      "definitions": [
        "normal core and finite quotient",
        "uniform measure and finite coset projection",
        "two-valued autocorrelation",
        "relative subgroup index and pointwise centering"
      ],
